    {
      "headline": "Development of Hawking Radiation Theory",
      "text": "In 1974, Stephen Hawking developed the theoretical model of Hawking radiation, which describes the emission of radiation from just outside a black hole's event horizon. This was a groundbreaking discovery as it contradicted previous models that assumed electromagnetic radiation could not escape from within the event horizon.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Hawking's Paper",
      "text": "In 1975, Stephen Hawking published a paper titled 'Particle Creation by Black Holes' in the journal Communications in Mathematical Physics. This paper detailed the theoretical underpinnings of Hawking radiation and its implications for black hole thermodynamics.",
      "date": "1975"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Impact on Black Hole Thermodynamics",
      "text": "Hawking radiation introduced the concept that black holes can lose mass and energy, leading to the possibility of black hole evaporation over time. This had significant implications for the laws of thermodynamics as applied to black holes.",
      "date": "1975"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ongoing Research and Debate",
      "text": "Since its introduction, Hawking radiation has been a topic of extensive research and debate within the scientific community. While it remains a theoretical prediction, it has influenced numerous studies in quantum mechanics and general relativity.",
      "date": "1975-Present"
    }
  ]
}

