      "headline": "First Full-Scale Working Railway Steam Locomotive",
      "text": "In 1804, Richard Trevithick built the first full-scale working railway steam locomotive. This marked a significant milestone in the development of steam-powered transportation.",
      "date": "1804"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Public Railway to Use Steam Locomotives",
      "text": "In 1825, the Stockton and Darlington Railway, the first public railway to use steam locomotives, opened in England. George Stephenson's Locomotion No. 1 was the first locomotive to run on this railway.",
      "date": "1825"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Opening of the Liverpool and Manchester Railway",
      "text": "The Liverpool and Manchester Railway, the first railway to rely exclusively on steam power, opened in 1830. It was also the first to have a double track and to carry both passengers and freight.",
      "date": "1830"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the 'Rocket' Locomotive",
      "text": "In 1829, George Stephenson and his son Robert designed the 'Rocket', which won the Rainhill Trials and set the standard for future steam locomotives.",
      "date": "1829"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion of Steam Locomotives in the United States",
      "text": "By the 1840s, steam locomotives had become the dominant form of transportation in the United States, facilitating westward expansion and economic growth.",
      "date": "1840"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak of Steam Locomotive Production",
      "text": "The early 20th century marked the peak of steam locomotive production, with thousands being built annually to meet the demands of expanding rail networks worldwide.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline of Steam Locomotives",
      "text": "By the mid-20th century, steam locomotives began to decline as diesel and electric locomotives became more efficient and cost-effective.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Steam Locomotive Use in Regular Service",
      "text": "The use of steam locomotives in regular service largely ended by the 1970s, although they remain popular for heritage railways and tourist attractions.",
      "date": "1970"
    }
  ]
}

