      "headline": "Homer Plessy's Arrest",
      "text": "On June 7, 1892, Homer Plessy, a mixed-race shoemaker, was arrested for refusing to leave a whites-only car on a train in New Orleans, Louisiana. This act was a deliberate challenge to Louisiana's segregation laws.",
      "date": "1892-06-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Plessy v. Ferguson Case Begins",
      "text": "The case of Plessy v. Ferguson was initiated in 1892 when Homer Plessy was charged with violating Louisiana's Separate Car Act.",
      "date": "1892"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Louisiana Supreme Court Decision",
      "text": "In 1893, the Louisiana Supreme Court upheld the state's segregation law, ruling against Homer Plessy.",
      "date": "1893"
    },
    {
      "headline": "U.S. Supreme Court Hearing",
      "text": "The U.S. Supreme Court heard arguments for Plessy v. Ferguson on April 13, 1896.",
      "date": "1896-04-13"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Plessy v. Ferguson Decision",
      "text": "On May 18, 1896, the U.S. Supreme Court delivered its decision in Plessy v. Ferguson, ruling 7-1 that racial segregation laws were constitutional under the 'separate but equal' doctrine.",
      "date": "1896-05-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Impact of Plessy v. Ferguson",
      "text": "The Plessy v. Ferguson decision legitimized the state laws establishing racial segregation, known as Jim Crow laws, which persisted in the American South until the civil rights movement of the 1960s.",
      "date": "1896"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Brown v. Board of Education",
      "text": "In 1954, the U.S. Supreme Court overturned Plessy v. Ferguson with its decision in Brown v. Board of Education, declaring that 'separate but equal' educational facilities were inherently unequal.",
      "date": "1954"
    }
  ]
}

