      "headline": "Emergence of Expressionism",
      "text": "Expressionism emerged as a modernist movement in poetry and painting in Northern Europe around the beginning of the 20th century. It was characterized by presenting the world from a subjective perspective, distorting it for emotional effect.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Die Brücke Formation",
      "text": "In 1905, the artist group Die Brücke was formed in Dresden, Germany. This group is often considered a key founding organization of Expressionism, focusing on emotional experience and vivid color.",
      "date": "1905"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Der Blaue Reiter Formation",
      "text": "In 1911, another influential Expressionist group, Der Blaue Reiter, was founded in Munich by artists including Wassily Kandinsky and Franz Marc. They emphasized spiritual and symbolic content in their work.",
      "date": "1911"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expressionism in Theatre",
      "text": "Expressionism began to influence theatre in the 1910s, with playwrights like Georg Kaiser and Ernst Toller creating works that emphasized the inner emotional experience over realistic representation.",
      "date": "1910"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expressionism in Film",
      "text": "The influence of Expressionism extended to film in the 1920s, with iconic films such as 'The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari' (1920) showcasing distorted sets and dramatic lighting to convey psychological tension.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak and Decline of Expressionism",
      "text": "Expressionism reached its peak in the 1920s but began to decline as other movements like Surrealism and Dadaism gained prominence. However, its impact continued to be felt in various art forms.",
      "date": "1920"
    }
  ]
}

