      "headline": "Coining of Spoon Theory",
      "text": "In 2003, Christine Miserandino coined the term 'Spoon Theory' in an essay. She used a handful of spoons as a metaphor to describe the limited energy available to people with chronic illnesses for daily activities.",
      "date": "2003"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Spoon Theory Essay",
      "text": "Christine Miserandino published her essay on Spoon Theory on her personal website, 'But You Don't Look Sick', explaining how she uses spoons to represent energy units for people with chronic illnesses.",
      "date": "2003"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption of Spoon Theory by Chronic Illness Community",
      "text": "Following its publication, Spoon Theory was widely adopted by the chronic illness community as a way to articulate the challenges of living with limited energy.",
      "date": "2000s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion of Spoon Theory Usage",
      "text": "Over the years, Spoon Theory has been used to describe not only chronic illnesses but also a range of disabilities, mental health issues, and other factors that might place unseen burdens on individuals.",
      "date": "2010s"
