      "headline": "Peak of Mycenaean Civilization",
      "text": "Around 1350 BCE, Mycenae reached its peak as a powerful city-state, known for its impressive architecture, including the Lion Gate and the royal palace.",
      "date": "-1350"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Construction of the Lion Gate",
      "text": "The Lion Gate, the main entrance to the citadel of Mycenae, was constructed around 1250 BCE. It is one of the most famous architectural features of Mycenaean Greece.",
      "date": "-1250"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Destruction of Mycenae",
      "text": "Around 1200 BCE, Mycenae suffered destruction, possibly due to earthquakes, invasions, or internal strife, marking the beginning of its decline.",
      "date": "-1200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Final Destruction and Abandonment",
      "text": "By 1100 BCE, Mycenae was finally destroyed and abandoned, marking the end of the Mycenaean civilization.",
      "date": "-1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rediscovery of Mycenae",
      "text": "In 1876, the archaeological site of Mycenae was rediscovered by Heinrich Schliemann, who conducted excavations that revealed the ancient city's significance.",
      "date": "1876"
    }
  ]
}

