    {
      "headline": "Earliest Mention of Mordred",
      "text": "The earliest known mention of a possibly historical Medraut (Mordred) is in the Welsh chronicle Annales Cambriae, wherein he and Arthur are ambiguously associated with the Battle of Camlann in a brief entry for the year 537.",
      "date": "0537"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Depiction in Historia Regum Britanniae",
      "text": "In the 12th century, Mordred is depicted as Arthur's traitorous nephew and a legitimate son of King Lot in the pseudo-historical work Historia Regum Britanniae by Geoffrey of Monmouth. This work served as the basis for the following evolution of the legend.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Characterization as Arthur's Bastard Son",
      "text": "Later variants of the legend most often characterized Mordred as Arthur's villainous bastard son, born of an incestuous relationship with his half-sister, the queen of Lothian or Orkney, named either Anna, Orcades, or Morgause.",
      "date": "1200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Camlann and Mordred's Death",
      "text": "The accounts presented in the Historia and most other versions include Mordred's death at the Battle of Camlann, typically in a final duel, during which he manages to mortally wound his own slayer, Arthur.",
      "date": "0537"
    }
  ]
}

