    {
      "headline": "William James Popularizes Pragmatism",
      "text": "In 1898, William James delivered a lecture titled 'Philosophical Conceptions and Practical Results' at the University of California, Berkeley. This lecture helped popularize pragmatism and introduced it to a broader audience.",
      "date": "1898"
    },
    {
      "headline": "John Dewey's Influence on Pragmatism",
      "text": "In the early 20th century, John Dewey became a leading figure in the pragmatist movement. His works, including 'Democracy and Education' (1916) and 'Experience and Nature' (1925), emphasized the role of education and experience in shaping human thought and action.",
      "date": "1916"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Pragmatism and Analytic Philosophy",
      "text": "During the mid-20th century, pragmatism influenced the development of analytic philosophy, particularly through the works of philosophers like W.V.O. Quine and Wilfrid Sellars, who integrated pragmatic ideas into their critiques of logical positivism.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Richard Rorty's Neo-Pragmatism",
      "text": "In the late 20th century, Richard Rorty emerged as a prominent neo-pragmatist. His book 'Philosophy and the Mirror of Nature' (1979) challenged traditional philosophical ideas and emphasized the contingency of language and the social construction of knowledge.",
      "date": "1979"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Continental Army",
      "text": "The Continental Army was formed on June 14, 1775, by a resolution passed by the Second Continental Congress, meeting in Philadelphia. It was created to coordinate the military efforts of the Thirteen Colonies in their war for independence against Great Britain.",
      "date": "1775-06-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "George Washington Appointed Commander-in-Chief",
      "text": "On June 15, 1775, General George Washington was appointed as the commander-in-chief of the Continental Army. He led the army throughout the American Revolutionary War.",
      "date": "1775-06-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Siege of Boston",
      "text": "The Continental Army's first major engagement was the Siege of Boston, which began in April 1775 and ended on March 17, 1776, with the British evacuation of the city.",
      "date": "1776-03-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Trenton",
      "text": "On December 26, 1776, the Continental Army achieved a significant victory at the Battle of Trenton, boosting morale after a series of defeats.",
      "date": "1776-12-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Saratoga",
      "text": "The Continental Army's victory at the Battle of Saratoga in October 1777 was a turning point in the war, convincing France to enter the conflict as an ally of the United States.",
      "date": "1777-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Winter at Valley Forge",
      "text": "During the winter of 1777-1778, the Continental Army endured harsh conditions at Valley Forge, where they underwent significant training and reorganization under the supervision of Baron von Steuben.",
      "date": "1777-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Monmouth",
      "text": "On June 28, 1778, the Continental Army fought the British at the Battle of Monmouth, which ended in a draw but demonstrated the improved discipline and effectiveness of the American forces.",
      "date": "1778-06-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Siege of Yorktown",
      "text": "The Siege of Yorktown, from September 28 to October 19, 1781, was the last major battle of the American Revolutionary War. The Continental Army, with French assistance, forced the surrender of British General Cornwallis.",
      "date": "1781-10-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Disbandment of the Continental Army",
      "text": "After the Treaty of Paris was signed in 1783, officially ending the war, the Continental Army was largely disbanded. On November 2, 1783, George Washington issued his farewell orders to the army.",
      "date": "1783-11-02"
    }
  ]
}

