      "headline": "Development of the Fitzpatrick Scale",
      "text": "In 1975, American dermatologist Thomas B. Fitzpatrick developed the Fitzpatrick scale as a way to estimate the response of different types of skin to ultraviolet (UV) light. It was initially developed on the basis of skin color to measure the correct dose of UVA for PUVA therapy.",
      "date": "1975"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modification of the Fitzpatrick Scale",
      "text": "The initial testing of the Fitzpatrick scale, which was based only on hair and eye color, resulted in too high UVA doses for some individuals. Consequently, it was altered to be based on the patient's reports of how their skin responds to the sun.",
      "date": "1975"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Extension of the Fitzpatrick Scale",
      "text": "The Fitzpatrick scale was extended to include a wider range of skin types, making it a more comprehensive tool for dermatological research into human skin pigmentation.",
      "date": "1975"
    }
  ]
}

