      "text": "The Liberty Bell was originally cast in 1752 by the Whitechapel Bell Foundry in London, England.",
      "date": "1752"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arrival in Philadelphia",
      "text": "The Liberty Bell arrived in Philadelphia in August 1752. Upon arrival, it cracked during a test strike.",
      "date": "1752-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recasting of the Bell",
      "text": "After cracking, the bell was recast twice in 1753 by local founders John Pass and John Stow.",
      "date": "1753"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Installation in the State House",
      "text": "The Liberty Bell was installed in the steeple of the Pennsylvania State House, now known as Independence Hall, in 1753.",
      "date": "1753"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Proclamation of the Declaration of Independence",
      "text": "Traditionally, it is believed that the Liberty Bell rang on July 8, 1776, to mark the reading of the Declaration of Independence.",
      "date": "1776-07-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Crack Appears in the Bell",
      "text": "The Liberty Bell developed a crack in the early 19th century, though the exact date is uncertain. It was repaired in 1846.",
      "date": "1846"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Final Ringing of the Liberty Bell",
      "text": "The Liberty Bell was rung for George Washington's birthday on February 23, 1846, after which the crack expanded, rendering it unringable.",
      "date": "1846-02-23"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Relocation to Liberty Bell Center",
      "text": "The Liberty Bell was moved to the Liberty Bell Center in Independence National Historical Park in 2003, where it remains today.",
      "date": "2003"
    }
  ]
}

