      "headline": "Construction of Al-Aqsa Mosque",
      "text": "The original Al-Aqsa Mosque was constructed by the Umayyad Caliph Abd al-Malik and completed by his son Al-Walid in 705 CE.",
      "date": "0705"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Earthquake and Reconstruction",
      "text": "In 746 CE, an earthquake destroyed the Al-Aqsa Mosque. It was rebuilt by the Abbasid Caliph Al-Mansur in 754 CE and further expanded by his successor Al-Mahdi in 780 CE.",
      "date": "0754"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Further Renovations",
      "text": "In 1033 CE, another earthquake damaged the mosque, leading to reconstruction efforts by the Fatimid Caliph Ali az-Zahir in 1035 CE.",
      "date": "1035"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Crusader Period",
      "text": "During the Crusader occupation of Jerusalem (1099-1187 CE), the Al-Aqsa Mosque was used as a palace and headquarters by the Knights Templar.",
      "date": "1099"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ayyubid Restoration",
      "text": "After recapturing Jerusalem in 1187 CE, Saladin restored the Al-Aqsa Mosque to its function as a mosque.",
      "date": "1187"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mamluk Period Enhancements",
      "text": "Between the 13th and 16th centuries, the Mamluks added various structures to the Al-Aqsa compound, including madrasas, minarets, and other religious buildings.",
      "date": "1300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ottoman Renovations",
      "text": "The Ottomans, who ruled from 1517 to 1917, undertook several renovations of the Al-Aqsa Mosque, including the restoration of the Dome of the Rock's tiles.",
      "date": "1517"
    },
    {
      "headline": "British Mandate Period",
      "text": "During the British Mandate (1920-1948), the Al-Aqsa Mosque underwent significant restoration work, including the replacement of the lead dome with an aluminum one.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Jordanian Rule and Renovations",
      "text": "From 1948 to 1967, under Jordanian control, the Al-Aqsa Mosque saw further renovations, including the installation of a new lead covering for the dome.",
      "date": "1948"
    },
    {
      "headline": "1969 Arson Attack",
      "text": "On August 21, 1969, a fire set by an Australian tourist damaged the southeastern wing of the Al-Aqsa Mosque, leading to international condemnation and restoration efforts.",
      "date": "1969-08-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Renovations",
      "text": "In recent decades, ongoing renovations have been conducted to preserve the structural integrity and historical significance of the Al-Aqsa Mosque.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

