      "headline": "Council of Chalcedon",
      "text": "The Council of Chalcedon was held in 451 AD. It was the fourth ecumenical council of the Christian Church and marked a significant event in the history of the Greek Orthodox Church, as it defined the two natures of Christ, both human and divine.",
      "date": "0451"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Great Schism",
      "text": "The Great Schism occurred in 1054 AD, leading to the separation between the Eastern Orthodox Church and the Roman Catholic Church. This event significantly shaped the identity of the Greek Orthodox Church.",
      "date": "1054"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fall of Constantinople",
      "text": "In 1453, Constantinople fell to the Ottoman Empire. This event marked the end of the Byzantine Empire and had a profound impact on the Greek Orthodox Church, as it came under Ottoman rule.",
      "date": "1453"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Church of Greece",
      "text": "The Church of Greece was established in 1833, following the Greek War of Independence. It became an autocephalous church, separate from the Ecumenical Patriarchate of Constantinople.",
      "date": "1833"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition of the Church of Cyprus",
      "text": "The Church of Cyprus was recognized as autocephalous in 431 AD at the Council of Ephesus, making it one of the oldest autocephalous churches in the Greek Orthodox tradition.",
      "date": "0431"
    }
  ]
}

