      "text": "The Servicemen's Readjustment Act of 1944, commonly known as the G.I. Bill, was signed into law by President Franklin D. Roosevelt on June 22, 1944. It provided a range of benefits to returning World War II veterans, including funding for education, housing, and unemployment compensation.",
      "date": "1944-06-22"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First G.I. Bill Benefits Distributed",
      "text": "The first benefits under the G.I. Bill began to be distributed in 1944, shortly after the law was enacted. These benefits included tuition payments for veterans attending college or vocational schools.",
      "date": "1944"
    },
    {
      "headline": "G.I. Bill's Impact on Higher Education",
      "text": "By 1947, veterans made up nearly half of the college enrollment in the United States, significantly increasing the number of Americans who attended college and contributing to the expansion of higher education institutions.",
      "date": "1947"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expiration of the Original G.I. Bill",
      "text": "The original G.I. Bill expired in 1956, after providing educational benefits to millions of veterans and significantly impacting American society by facilitating the transition of veterans to civilian life.",
      "date": "1956"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-9/11 Veterans Educational Assistance Act",
      "text": "In 2008, the Post-9/11 Veterans Educational Assistance Act, often referred to as the Post-9/11 G.I. Bill, was enacted. It expanded educational benefits for veterans who served after September 11, 2001, including covering the full cost of in-state tuition and fees at public colleges and universities.",
      "date": "2008"
    }
  ]
}

