      "headline": "Existence of Archaeopteryx",
      "text": "Archaeopteryx lived during the Late Jurassic period, approximately 150 million years ago. It is one of the earliest known birds, showcasing a mix of avian and dinosaurian features.",
      "date": "-0150000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of First Archaeopteryx Fossil",
      "text": "The first Archaeopteryx fossil was discovered in 1861 in the Solnhofen limestone in Bavaria, Germany. This fossil was a single feather, which was initially thought to belong to a bird.",
      "date": "1861"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of the London Specimen",
      "text": "In 1861, shortly after the feather was discovered, the first complete skeleton of Archaeopteryx, known as the London Specimen, was found. It provided crucial evidence of the link between birds and dinosaurs.",
      "date": "1861"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition as the Oldest Known Bird",
      "text": "Throughout the late 19th century and into the early 21st century, Archaeopteryx was widely recognized as the oldest known bird due to its combination of avian and reptilian features.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reevaluation of Archaeopteryx's Status",
      "text": "In the early 21st century, new discoveries of older avialans such as Anchiornis, Xiaotingia, and Aurornis led to a reevaluation of Archaeopteryx's status as the oldest known bird.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

