      "headline": "First Report of Alpha-gal Syndrome",
      "text": "Alpha-gal syndrome was first reported in 2002. It is characterized by a delayed allergic reaction to mammalian meat, resulting from past exposure to certain tick bites.",
      "date": "2002"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Identification of Alpha-gal as Allergen",
      "text": "Researchers identified the carbohydrate galactose-alpha-1,3-galactose (alpha-gal) as the allergen responsible for the syndrome. This discovery helped in understanding the mechanism behind the allergy.",
      "date": "2008"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Link to Tick Bites Established",
      "text": "Studies established a link between bites from certain ticks, such as the Lone Star tick in the United States, and the development of alpha-gal syndrome. This connection was crucial in identifying the cause of the allergy.",
      "date": "2009"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition of Delayed Reaction",
      "text": "Medical professionals recognized that the allergic reaction to alpha-gal is unique due to its delayed onset, typically occurring 3-6 hours after consuming mammalian meat.",
      "date": "2010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Increased Awareness and Research",
      "text": "Throughout the 2010s, awareness of alpha-gal syndrome increased, leading to more research and better diagnostic methods. This period saw a rise in reported cases, particularly in regions with high tick populations.",
      "date": "2010-2020"
    }
  ]
}

