      "headline": "Composition of Manusmriti",
      "text": "The Manusmriti, also known as the Mānava-Dharmaśāstra, is believed to have been composed between 200 BCE and 200 CE. It is one of the earliest and most authoritative texts on Hindu law and dharma.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Influence on Hindu Law",
      "text": "Throughout the centuries, the Manusmriti has significantly influenced Hindu law and social customs. It has been used as a foundational text for understanding dharma and social order in Hindu society.",
      "date": "0000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "British Colonial Period",
      "text": "During the British colonial period in India, the Manusmriti was used by British administrators to understand and codify Hindu law. This period saw the text being interpreted and applied in new legal contexts.",
      "date": "1772"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Criticism and Reform Movements",
      "text": "In the 19th and 20th centuries, the Manusmriti faced criticism from various social reformers and movements in India for its views on caste and gender. Reformers like B.R. Ambedkar criticized its role in perpetuating social inequalities.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

