      "text": "Porcelain was first developed in China during the Tang dynasty (618–907). It was characterized by its white, translucent quality and was made from kaolin and petuntse.",
      "date": "0618"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mature Porcelain in the Song Dynasty",
      "text": "During the Song dynasty (960–1279), porcelain production techniques were refined, leading to the creation of highly prized wares such as Ding, Ru, and Jun wares.",
      "date": "0960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Export of Porcelain",
      "text": "By the Yuan dynasty (1271–1368), Chinese porcelain was being exported to the Islamic world and Europe, becoming a highly sought-after commodity.",
      "date": "1271"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Porcelain in the Ming Dynasty",
      "text": "The Ming dynasty (1368–1644) saw the peak of porcelain production in China, with the famous blue and white porcelain becoming popular.",
      "date": "1368"
    },
    {
      "headline": "European Porcelain Production",
      "text": "In 1708, European porcelain production began in Meissen, Germany, marking the first successful attempt to replicate Chinese porcelain in Europe.",
      "date": "1708"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Industrial Use of Porcelain",
      "text": "In the 19th century, porcelain began to be used in industrial applications, such as electrical insulators and laboratory equipment, due to its insulating properties.",
      "date": "1800"
    }
  ]
}

