      "headline": "Origins of Matka Gambling",
      "text": "Matka gambling, originally known as Ankada Jugar, involved betting on the opening and closing rates of cotton transmitted from the New York Cotton Exchange to the Bombay Cotton Exchange. This practice dates back to before the Partition of India in 1947.",
      "date": "-1947"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transformation in the 1960s",
      "text": "In the 1960s, the original system of betting on cotton rates was replaced with other methods of generating random numbers. This included pulling slips from a large earthenware pot known as a matka, or using playing cards.",
      "date": "1960"
    }
  ]
}

