      "headline": "Invention of Numerical Control",
      "text": "The concept of numerical control (NC) was first introduced in the late 1940s by John T. Parsons, who developed a method to produce complex aircraft parts using punched card technology.",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the First NC Machine",
      "text": "In 1952, the first NC machine was developed at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) under the direction of John T. Parsons and Frank L. Stulen. This machine was a milling machine that used punched tape to control its movements.",
      "date": "1952"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Computer Numerical Control (CNC)",
      "text": "In the late 1960s, the development of mini-computers led to the introduction of computer numerical control (CNC), which replaced the punched tape with computer programs.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Commercialization of CNC Machines",
      "text": "By the 1970s, CNC machines became commercially available and began to be widely adopted in manufacturing industries for their precision and efficiency.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Advancements in CNC Technology",
      "text": "Throughout the 1980s and 1990s, CNC technology continued to advance with the integration of CAD/CAM software, allowing for more complex designs and automation.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of 3D Printing",
      "text": "In the 2000s, CNC technology expanded to include 3D printing, which uses additive manufacturing processes to create three-dimensional objects from digital models.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Hippocrates' Theory of Humourism",
      "text": "Hippocrates, a Greek physician, described the four temperaments as part of the ancient medical concept of humourism. He proposed that four bodily fluids (blood, yellow bile, black bile, and phlegm) affect human personality traits and behaviors. This theory laid the foundation for the four temperament theory.",
      "date": "-0460"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Galen's Expansion of the Four Temperaments",
      "text": "Galen, a prominent Greek physician and philosopher, expanded on Hippocrates' theory in the 2nd century AD. He associated the four temperaments with specific personality traits: sanguine (optimistic and social), choleric (short-tempered and irritable), melancholic (analytical and quiet), and phlegmatic (relaxed and peaceful).",
      "date": "0130"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Medieval and Renaissance Adoption",
      "text": "During the Medieval and Renaissance periods, the four temperaments were widely adopted in medical and psychological theories. They were used to explain human behavior and personality, influencing various aspects of culture, including literature and art.",
      "date": "1400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline of Humourism",
      "text": "With the advancement of modern medical science, the humourism theory, including the four temperaments, began to decline. The discovery of the circulatory system and the development of modern psychology led to a more scientific understanding of human physiology and behavior.",
      "date": "1700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Psychological Adaptations",
      "text": "In the 20th century, some psychological personality type systems, such as those developed by Carl Jung and the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, incorporated concepts similar to the Greek temperaments, although they do not define a fixed relationship between internal secretions and personality.",
      "date": "1920"
    }
  ]
}

