      "headline": "Origins of the Plymouth Brethren",
      "text": "The Plymouth Brethren originated in Dublin, Ireland, in the mid to late 1820s. The movement began as a reaction against the established church and emphasized a return to the simplicity of New Testament Christianity.",
      "date": "1820"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Meeting in Dublin",
      "text": "The first meeting of what would become the Plymouth Brethren took place in Dublin in 1827. This gathering marked the beginning of the movement's formal organization.",
      "date": "1827"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion to Plymouth, England",
      "text": "By 1831, the movement had expanded to Plymouth, England, where it gained significant traction and from which it derived its name, the Plymouth Brethren.",
      "date": "1831"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Division into Open and Exclusive Brethren",
      "text": "In 1848, a significant division occurred within the Plymouth Brethren, resulting in the formation of two main branches: the Open Brethren and the Exclusive Brethren. This split was primarily due to differing views on church governance and fellowship.",
      "date": "1848"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Growth and Global Spread",
      "text": "Throughout the late 19th and early 20th centuries, the Plymouth Brethren experienced growth and spread globally, establishing assemblies in North America, Australia, and other parts of the world.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Continued Influence and Diversity",
      "text": "In the 21st century, the Plymouth Brethren continue to exist as a diverse network of assemblies, with varying practices and degrees of separation from mainstream society, particularly among the Exclusive Brethren.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

