      "headline": "Construction of the Appian Way Begins",
      "text": "In 312 BC, the construction of the Appian Way was initiated by Appius Claudius Caecus, the Roman censor. It was built as a military road during the Samnite Wars to connect Rome to the south.",
      "date": "-0312"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of the First Section",
      "text": "The first section of the Appian Way was completed in 312 BC, connecting Rome to Capua. This section was crucial for military logistics and movement.",
      "date": "-0312"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Extension to Beneventum",
      "text": "By 268 BC, the Appian Way was extended to Beneventum, further enhancing its strategic importance for the Roman Republic.",
      "date": "-0268"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Extension to Tarentum and Brundisium",
      "text": "The road was further extended to Tarentum and Brundisium by 190 BC, completing its route to the southeastern coast of Italy and solidifying its role as a major trade and military route.",
      "date": "-0190"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Statius Refers to the Appian Way",
      "text": "The poet Statius referred to the Appian Way as 'Appia longarum... regina viarum', meaning 'the Appian Way, the queen of the long roads', highlighting its significance in Roman infrastructure.",
      "date": "0000"
    }
  ]
}

