      "headline": "October in the Roman Calendar",
      "text": "October was originally the eighth month in the old calendar of Romulus around 750 BC. It retained its name after January and February were inserted into the calendar that had originally been created by the Romans.",
      "date": "-0750"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mundus Patet",
      "text": "In Ancient Rome, one of the three Mundus patet ceremonies would take place on October 5. This was a day when the mundus, a pit thought to be a portal to the underworld, was opened.",
      "date": "-0750-10-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Meditrinalia",
      "text": "Meditrinalia was celebrated on October 11 in Ancient Rome. It was a festival in honor of the new wine and the goddess Meditrina.",
      "date": "-0750-10-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Augustalia",
      "text": "Augustalia was celebrated on October 12 in Ancient Rome, a festival in honor of Emperor Augustus.",
      "date": "-0750-10-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "October Horse",
      "text": "The October Horse was a festival held on October 15 in Ancient Rome, involving a chariot race and the sacrifice of a horse.",
      "date": "-0750-10-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Armilustrium",
      "text": "Armilustrium was celebrated on October 19 in Ancient Rome. It was a purification festival for the army, held in honor of Mars, the god of war.",
      "date": "-0750-10-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Winterfylleth",
      "text": "Among the Anglo-Saxons, October was known as Winterfylleth (Ƿinterfylleþ), because at this full moon, winter was supposed to begin.",
      "date": "-0500"
    }
  ]
}

