{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Initial Discovery of Liquid Crystals",
      "text": "In 1888, Austrian botanist Friedrich Reinitzer discovered liquid crystals while studying cholesterol extracted from carrots. He observed that the substance had two distinct melting points and exhibited unusual optical properties.",
      "date": "1888"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the First Liquid Crystal Display",
      "text": "In 1968, the first liquid crystal display (LCD) was developed by RCA (Radio Corporation of America) under the leadership of George H. Heilmeier. This early LCD was based on dynamic scattering mode (DSM) of liquid crystals.",
      "date": "1968"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Twisted Nematic Field Effect",
      "text": "In 1970, Wolfgang Helfrich and Martin Schadt at the Central Research Laboratories of Hoffmann-LaRoche in Switzerland invented the twisted nematic field effect, which became the basis for modern LCD technology.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Commercial LCD Watches",
      "text": "In 1972, the first commercial LCD watches were introduced by the American company Hamilton Watch Company and Japanese company Seiko, utilizing the twisted nematic field effect.",
      "date": "1972"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Active-Matrix LCDs",
      "text": "In the late 1980s, active-matrix LCDs, which use thin-film transistors (TFTs) to improve image quality and response time, were introduced. This technology became standard for computer monitors and televisions.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread Adoption of LCDs in Consumer Electronics",
      "text": "By the 2000s, LCDs had become the dominant display technology for televisions, computer monitors, and portable devices due to their compact size, low power consumption, and improved image quality.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
