{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Roman Law Development",
      "text": "Roman law began to develop as a legal system during the Roman Republic and continued to evolve through the Roman Empire. It laid the foundation for civil law systems.",
      "date": "-0509"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Corpus Juris Civilis",
      "text": "In 529-534, Emperor Justinian I of the Byzantine Empire ordered the compilation of Roman law, resulting in the Corpus Juris Civilis, which became a foundational document for civil law systems.",
      "date": "0529"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revival of Roman Law",
      "text": "In the 11th century, there was a revival of Roman law in Europe, particularly in Italy, where scholars began to study and teach Roman law at universities.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Napoleonic Code Enacted",
      "text": "On March 21, 1804, the Napoleonic Code was enacted in France. It was a comprehensive codification of civil law and became a model for legal systems in many other countries.",
      "date": "1804-03-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spread of Civil Law",
      "text": "Throughout the 19th and 20th centuries, the Napoleonic Code and other civil law systems spread to various parts of the world, including Latin America, Africa, and Asia, becoming the most widespread legal system globally.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Civil Law Today",
      "text": "As of the 21st century, civil law is the world's most common legal system, practiced in about 150 countries, characterized by comprehensive legal codes and less reliance on judicial precedent.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

{
