      "headline": "Freedom Summer Campaign",
      "text": "In 1964, James Chaney, Andrew Goodman, and Michael Schwerner participated in the Freedom Summer campaign, which aimed to register African Americans in Mississippi to vote.",
      "date": "1964"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arrest for Speeding",
      "text": "On June 21, 1964, James Chaney, Andrew Goodman, and Michael Schwerner were arrested for speeding in Neshoba County, Mississippi.",
      "date": "1964-06-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Release from Jail",
      "text": "Later on June 21, 1964, after being held for several hours, Chaney, Goodman, and Schwerner were released from jail.",
      "date": "1964-06-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Abduction and Murder",
      "text": "After their release on June 21, 1964, the three activists were followed by local law enforcement and members of the Ku Klux Klan, abducted, and taken to a secluded location where they were shot and killed.",
      "date": "1964-06-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bodies Discovered",
      "text": "The bodies of Chaney, Goodman, and Schwerner were discovered on August 4, 1964, buried in an earthen dam.",
      "date": "1964-08-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Federal Investigation and Trials",
      "text": "The murders led to a federal investigation, and in 1967, seven men were convicted of conspiracy to violate the civil rights of the three activists.",
      "date": "1967"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Edgar Ray Killen Convicted",
      "text": "On June 21, 2005, Edgar Ray Killen, a former Ku Klux Klan organizer, was convicted of manslaughter for his role in the murders.",
      "date": "2005-06-21"
    }
  ]
}

