      "headline": "Invention of the Trebuchet",
      "text": "The traction trebuchet, also known as a mangonel in some sources, is believed to have been invented in China around the 4th century BC. It was operated by a team of people pulling on ropes attached to a lever arm.",
      "date": "-0400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction to the Mediterranean",
      "text": "The traction trebuchet was introduced to the Mediterranean by the Avars, a nomadic group, around the 6th century AD. This introduction marked the spread of the technology into Europe.",
      "date": "0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Counterweight Trebuchet",
      "text": "The counterweight trebuchet, which uses a counterweight to swing the arm, was developed in the Mediterranean region around the 12th century. This design allowed for greater power and accuracy.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Use in the Siege of Acre",
      "text": "During the Siege of Acre in 1189-1191, the counterweight trebuchet was used effectively by both the Crusaders and the Muslim defenders, demonstrating its power in medieval warfare.",
      "date": "1189"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trebuchet in the Siege of Stirling Castle",
      "text": "In 1304, during the Siege of Stirling Castle, the largest trebuchet ever built, known as the Warwolf, was constructed by order of King Edward I of England. It was capable of hurling massive stones with great force.",
      "date": "1304"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline of the Trebuchet",
      "text": "By the 15th century, the development and use of gunpowder artillery led to the decline of the trebuchet as a siege weapon, as cannons became more effective and easier to use.",
      "date": "1400"
    }
  ]
}

