      "headline": "Frank Benford's Rediscovery",
      "text": "In 1938, physicist Frank Benford rediscovered the phenomenon and tested it on a much larger set of data. He published a paper titled 'The Law of Anomalous Numbers' in the Proceedings of the American Philosophical Society, which included data from 20 different domains.",
      "date": "1938"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Benford's Law Gains Recognition",
      "text": "Throughout the late 20th century, Benford's law gained recognition as a useful tool in fields such as accounting and fraud detection, as it could be used to detect anomalies in datasets that should conform to the expected distribution of leading digits.",
      "date": "1970s-1990s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Application in Digital Forensics",
      "text": "In the early 21st century, Benford's law began to be applied in digital forensics and data science for detecting anomalies and potential fraud in large datasets, such as financial records and election data.",
      "date": "2000s"
    }
  ]
}

