      "text": "The earliest known cave paintings are found in the El Castillo cave in Spain, dating back to at least 40,800 years ago. These paintings are attributed to either early Homo sapiens or Neanderthals.",
      "date": "-038000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Chauvet Cave Paintings",
      "text": "The Chauvet Cave in France contains some of the best-preserved figurative cave paintings in the world, dating back to around 30,000 to 32,000 years ago.",
      "date": "-030000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lascaux Cave Paintings",
      "text": "The Lascaux Caves in France are famous for their Paleolithic cave paintings, estimated to be around 17,000 years old. These paintings include images of large animals once native to the region.",
      "date": "-017000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Altamira Cave Paintings",
      "text": "The Altamira Cave in Spain is renowned for its Upper Paleolithic cave paintings, which date back to around 36,000 years ago. These paintings are notable for their vivid colors and dynamic depictions of animals.",
      "date": "-036000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sulawesi Cave Paintings",
      "text": "Cave paintings on the Indonesian island of Sulawesi have been dated to at least 43,900 years ago, making them some of the oldest known figurative art in the world.",
      "date": "-043900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cave Paintings in the Americas",
      "text": "Cave paintings in the Americas, such as those in the Cueva de las Manos in Argentina, date back to around 9,000 to 13,000 years ago, showcasing hand stencils and hunting scenes.",
      "date": "-009000"
    }
  ]
}

