    },
    {
      "headline": "Mexican Revolution",
      "text": "Ciudad Juárez played a significant role during the Mexican Revolution, particularly in 1911 when revolutionary forces led by Francisco I. Madero captured the city, marking a turning point in the revolution.",
      "date": "1911"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bracero Program",
      "text": "From 1942 to 1964, the Bracero Program brought many Mexican laborers to the United States through Ciudad Juárez, impacting the city's economy and demographics.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rise of Maquiladoras",
      "text": "In the 1960s, Ciudad Juárez saw the establishment of maquiladoras, or manufacturing plants, which significantly boosted the local economy and population growth.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Violence and Drug War",
      "text": "In the late 2000s, Ciudad Juárez became one of the most dangerous cities in the world due to drug cartel violence, with a peak in homicides occurring in 2010.",
      "date": "2010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Economic Recovery",
      "text": "By the mid-2010s, Ciudad Juárez began to recover economically and socially, with a decrease in violence and an increase in foreign investment.",
