      "headline": "Establishment of Gran Colombia",
      "text": "Gran Colombia was established in December 1819 after the Congress of Angostura, where the Republic of Colombia was created, encompassing the territories of present-day Colombia, Venezuela, Ecuador, and Panama.",
      "date": "1819-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Simón Bolívar Elected President",
      "text": "Simón Bolívar was elected as the first president of Gran Colombia in 1819, following its establishment.",
      "date": "1819"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Carabobo",
      "text": "On June 24, 1821, the Battle of Carabobo took place, leading to a decisive victory for the forces of Gran Colombia over the Spanish Royalists, which was crucial for the independence of Venezuela.",
      "date": "1821-06-24"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cúcuta Congress",
      "text": "The Congress of Cúcuta was convened in 1821, where the Constitution of Cúcuta was adopted, officially unifying the territories of Gran Colombia under a central government.",
      "date": "1821"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Pichincha",
      "text": "On May 24, 1822, the Battle of Pichincha was fought near Quito, leading to the liberation of Ecuador from Spanish rule and its incorporation into Gran Colombia.",
      "date": "1822-05-24"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Ayacucho",
      "text": "On December 9, 1824, the Battle of Ayacucho was fought in Peru, marking the end of Spanish rule in South America and securing the independence of Peru, with Gran Colombia playing a significant role.",
      "date": "1824-12-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bolívar's Dictatorship",
      "text": "In 1828, Simón Bolívar declared himself dictator of Gran Colombia in an attempt to maintain the unity of the republic amidst growing regional tensions.",
      "date": "1828"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Dissolution of Gran Colombia",
      "text": "Gran Colombia was officially dissolved in 1831 due to political differences and regional tensions, leading to the formation of the independent nations of Venezuela, Ecuador, and New Granada (later Colombia).",
      "date": "1831"
    }
  ]
}

