      "headline": "Proposal of the Amendment",
      "text": "The Twenty-seventh Amendment was originally proposed on September 25, 1789, as part of the original twelve amendments to the United States Constitution, known as the Bill of Rights.",
      "date": "1789-09-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Initial Ratification by States",
      "text": "By the end of 1791, only six states had ratified the amendment, far short of the required three-fourths of the states needed for it to become part of the Constitution.",
      "date": "1791"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renewed Interest in the Amendment",
      "text": "Interest in the amendment was revived in the late 20th century, largely due to the efforts of Gregory Watson, a University of Texas undergraduate student, who began a campaign to complete its ratification in 1982.",
      "date": "1982"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ratification by Michigan",
      "text": "Michigan ratified the amendment on May 7, 1992, becoming the 38th state to do so, thus meeting the constitutional requirement for ratification.",
      "date": "1992-05-07"
    },
    {
