      "headline": "Earliest Evidence of Shaktism",
      "text": "The earliest evidence of Shaktism is found in the Indus Valley Civilization, where terracotta figurines of goddesses have been discovered, dating back to around 3000-1500 BCE.",
      "date": "-3000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Vedic Period References",
      "text": "During the Vedic period (1500-500 BCE), references to goddesses such as Ushas and Prithvi can be found, indicating early forms of goddess worship.",
      "date": "-1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Composition of Devi Upanishad",
      "text": "The Devi Upanishad, a text dedicated to the goddess, is composed around 6th century BCE, highlighting the philosophical aspects of Shaktism.",
      "date": "-0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Tantric Traditions",
      "text": "Between the 4th and 6th centuries CE, Tantric traditions that emphasize the worship of the goddess begin to develop, influencing Shaktism significantly.",
      "date": "0400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Composition of Devi Mahatmya",
      "text": "The Devi Mahatmya, a key text in Shaktism, is composed around the 6th century CE. It is part of the Markandeya Purana and celebrates the goddess as the supreme power.",
      "date": "0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rise of Shakta Pithas",
      "text": "By the 7th century CE, Shakta Pithas, or sacred sites dedicated to the goddess, become prominent across India, serving as important centers of Shaktism.",
      "date": "0700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Influence of Adi Shankaracharya",
      "text": "In the 8th century CE, Adi Shankaracharya, a key figure in Hindu philosophy, integrates Shaktism into the broader framework of Advaita Vedanta.",
      "date": "0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Composition of Lalita Sahasranama",
      "text": "The Lalita Sahasranama, a text listing the thousand names of the goddess Lalita, is composed around the 11th century CE, becoming an important liturgical text in Shaktism.",
      "date": "1000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Vijayanagara Empire Patronage",
      "text": "During the 14th to 16th centuries CE, the Vijayanagara Empire in South India becomes a major patron of Shaktism, promoting the worship of goddesses like Durga and Lakshmi.",
      "date": "1300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Revival and Global Spread",
      "text": "In the 20th and 21st centuries, Shaktism experiences a revival and spreads globally, with increased interest in goddess worship and Tantric practices.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

