      "headline": "Creation of the FBI Indexes",
      "text": "The FBI Indexes were created as a system to track American citizens and other individuals deemed dangerous to national security. This system was initially developed by J. Edgar Hoover at the Bureau of Investigations before he became the director of the FBI.",
      "date": "1920s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "J. Edgar Hoover Appointed Director of the FBI",
      "text": "J. Edgar Hoover was appointed as the first Director of the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) in 1924. Under his leadership, the FBI Indexes were expanded and utilized extensively.",
      "date": "1924"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion of the FBI Indexes",
      "text": "Throughout the 1930s and 1940s, the FBI Indexes expanded to include various divisions, each rating individuals based on the perceived level of threat they posed to national security.",
      "date": "1930s-1940s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Use of the FBI Indexes During World War II",
      "text": "During World War II, the FBI Indexes were heavily used to monitor individuals suspected of espionage or subversion, particularly those with ties to enemy nations.",
      "date": "1941-1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transition to Computerized Databases",
      "text": "With advancements in technology, the FBI began transitioning from paper index cards to computerized databases, eventually phasing out the traditional FBI Indexes.",
      "date": "1970s"
    }
  ]
}

{
