      "headline": "Madrid Train Bombings",
      "text": "On the morning of 11 March 2004, a series of coordinated bombings targeted the Cercanías commuter train system in Madrid, Spain. The attacks resulted in the deaths of 193 people and injured around 2,000 others. This was the deadliest terrorist attack in Spain's history and the deadliest in Europe since 1988.",
      "date": "2004-03-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Immediate Aftermath and Investigation",
      "text": "In the immediate aftermath of the bombings, Spanish authorities launched an investigation to determine the perpetrators. Initial suspicion fell on the Basque separatist group ETA, but evidence soon pointed to Islamist extremists.",
      "date": "2004-03-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Political Impact",
      "text": "The bombings occurred just three days before Spain's general elections on 14 March 2004. The attacks had a significant impact on the elections, contributing to the defeat of the incumbent government led by the People's Party, which had supported the US-led invasion of Iraq.",
      "date": "2004-03-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arrests and Trials",
      "text": "In the months following the bombings, Spanish police arrested several individuals linked to the attacks. In 2007, 29 people were tried, and 21 were convicted for their roles in the bombings.",
      "date": "2007"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Convictions and Sentences",
      "text": "On 31 October 2007, the Spanish court issued its verdict, convicting 21 individuals for their involvement in the bombings. The sentences ranged from several years to thousands of years in prison, reflecting the severity of the crimes.",
      "date": "2007-10-31"
    }
  ]
}

