    {
      "headline": "Origin of the Epistolary Novel",
      "text": "The term 'epistolary' is derived from the Latin word 'epistola', which comes from the Greek word 'epistolē', meaning a letter. This form of novel writing began to take shape as a distinct genre.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Pamela, or Virtue Rewarded'",
      "text": "In 1740, Samuel Richardson published 'Pamela, or Virtue Rewarded', which is considered one of the first true epistolary novels. The novel is composed entirely of letters written by the protagonist, Pamela Andrews.",
      "date": "1740"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Clarissa'",
      "text": "In 1748, Samuel Richardson published 'Clarissa', another significant epistolary novel. It is one of the longest novels in the English language and is composed of letters written by several characters.",
      "date": "1748"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Les Liaisons dangereuses'",
      "text": "In 1782, Pierre Choderlos de Laclos published 'Les Liaisons dangereuses', a French epistolary novel that became famous for its exploration of moral ambiguity and manipulation.",
