      "text": "In 1947, the Republic of China (ROC) government published a map with an eleven-dash line, marking its claims in the South China Sea. This map included the Paracel Islands, Spratly Islands, Pratas Island, Macclesfield Bank, and Scarborough Shoal.",
      "date": "1947"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reduction to Nine-Dash Line",
      "text": "In 1952, the People's Republic of China (PRC) reduced the eleven-dash line to nine dashes by removing two dashes in the Gulf of Tonkin.",
      "date": "1952"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Inclusion in International Maps",
      "text": "The nine-dash line began appearing in international maps and documents, marking the PRC's claims in the South China Sea.",
      "date": "1950s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "UNCLOS and South China Sea Disputes",
      "text": "The United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) came into effect in 1994, leading to increased disputes over maritime boundaries, including those marked by the nine-dash line.",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Philippines v. China Arbitration Case",
      "text": "On July 12, 2016, the Permanent Court of Arbitration ruled in favor of the Philippines, stating that China's claims based on the nine-dash line had no legal basis under international law.",
      "date": "2016-07-12"
    }
  ]
}

