      "headline": "Introduction of the LP Record",
      "text": "The LP (Long Play) record was introduced by Columbia Records in 1948. It featured a speed of 33+1⁄3 rpm, a 12- or 10-inch diameter, and used the 'microgroove' groove specification.",
      "date": "1948"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption as Industry Standard",
      "text": "Following its introduction, the LP record was soon adopted as a new standard by the entire US record industry.",
      "date": "1948"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Stereophonic Sound",
      "text": "In 1957, the LP format saw the important addition of stereophonic sound, enhancing the listening experience.",
      "date": "1957"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Competition with RCA Victor's '45' Format",
      "text": "The LP format competed against the smaller 7-inch sized '45' or 'single' format by RCA Victor, eventually becoming the dominant format.",
      "date": "1950s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Album Era",
      "text": "The LP remained the standard format for record albums during a period in popular music known as the album era, which spanned from the 1960s to the 1980s.",
      "date": "1960s-1980s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Vinyl Revival Era",
      "text": "In the modern era, known as the vinyl revival, a large majority of records are based on the LP format, and the LP name continues to be in use today to refer to new records.",
      "date": "2000s-present"
    }
  ]
}

