      "headline": "Prague Spring Begins",
      "text": "In early 1968, the Prague Spring began as a period of political liberalization in Czechoslovakia under the leadership of Alexander Dubček.",
      "date": "1968"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Warsaw Pact Invasion",
      "text": "On the night of 20–21 August 1968, the Czechoslovak Socialist Republic was invaded by four Warsaw Pact countries: the Soviet Union, the Polish People's Republic, the People's Republic of Bulgaria, and the Hungarian People's Republic.",
      "date": "1968-08-20"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Occupation of Prague",
      "text": "By the morning of 21 August 1968, the invading forces had occupied Prague and other key areas, effectively ending the Prague Spring.",
      "date": "1968-08-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Prague Spring Reforms",
      "text": "The invasion stopped Alexander Dubček's Prague Spring liberalisation reforms and led to the strengthening of the authoritarian wing of the Communist Party of Czechoslovakia (KSČ).",
      "date": "1968-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Brezhnev Doctrine Announced",
      "text": "In the aftermath of the invasion, the Soviet Union announced the Brezhnev Doctrine, which justified the intervention by asserting the right to intervene in any socialist country that was deemed to be threatened by internal or external forces hostile to socialism.",
      "date": "1968"
    }
  ]
}

