      "headline": "Origins of Reflexology",
      "text": "The practice of applying pressure to certain points on the body can be traced back to ancient civilizations. Evidence suggests that forms of reflexology were practiced in China and Egypt as early as 2330 BCE.",
      "date": "-2330"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Zone Therapy Introduction",
      "text": "In the early 20th century, Dr. William H. Fitzgerald, an American ear, nose, and throat specialist, introduced 'zone therapy' to the United States. He proposed that applying pressure to certain areas of the body could have an anesthetic effect on other parts.",
      "date": "1915"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development by Eunice D. Ingham",
      "text": "Eunice D. Ingham, a nurse and physiotherapist, further developed the concept of reflexology in the 1930s. She mapped the entire body onto the feet and hands, creating the modern reflexology charts.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Stories the Feet Can Tell'",
      "text": "In 1938, Eunice D. Ingham published 'Stories the Feet Can Tell', a book that detailed her findings and the practice of reflexology.",
      "date": "1938"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the International Institute of Reflexology",
      "text": "In 1973, the International Institute of Reflexology was established to promote and teach the practice of reflexology worldwide.",
      "date": "1973"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition and Popularity",
      "text": "Throughout the late 20th and early 21st centuries, reflexology gained popularity as a complementary therapy, despite its classification as a pseudoscience by the medical community.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

