      "headline": "Origin of Necklacing",
      "text": "Necklacing originated in the 1980s in black townships of apartheid South Africa. It was used as a method of extrajudicial execution and torture, primarily against suspected apartheid collaborators.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Known Use",
      "text": "The first known use of necklacing as a form of execution occurred in the early 1980s in South Africa. It became a symbol of the violent struggle against apartheid.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread Use During Apartheid",
      "text": "Throughout the 1980s, necklacing was used widely in South Africa during the struggle against apartheid. It was often carried out in public as a warning to others.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "International Attention",
      "text": "The brutal nature of necklacing drew international attention and condemnation. It highlighted the intense violence and desperation within South African townships during apartheid.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline in Use",
      "text": "After the end of apartheid in the early 1990s, the use of necklacing as a form of execution declined significantly as South Africa transitioned to a democratic government.",
      "date": "1990"
    }
  ]
}

