      "headline": "First Conceptualization of Scramjet",
      "text": "The concept of a scramjet, or supersonic combustion ramjet, was first proposed in the 1950s. This idea was based on the principle of allowing combustion to occur in supersonic airflow, which would enable efficient propulsion at extremely high speeds.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Successful Ground Test",
      "text": "In 1964, the first successful ground test of a scramjet engine was conducted by the United States Air Force. This test demonstrated the feasibility of supersonic combustion for propulsion.",
      "date": "1964"
    },
    {
      "headline": "NASA's Hypersonic Research",
      "text": "During the 1980s and 1990s, NASA conducted extensive research into hypersonic flight and scramjet technology, leading to the development of experimental vehicles such as the X-43.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "X-43A Sets Speed Record",
      "text": "On March 27, 2004, NASA's X-43A scramjet-powered aircraft set a world speed record for an air-breathing vehicle, reaching Mach 7 (approximately 4,900 mph or 7,900 km/h).",
      "date": "2004-03-27"
    },
    {
      "headline": "X-43A Breaks Its Own Record",
      "text": "On November 16, 2004, the X-43A broke its own speed record by reaching Mach 9.6 (approximately 7,310 mph or 11,760 km/h), further proving the potential of scramjet technology.",
      "date": "2004-11-16"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Boeing X-51 Waverider Test",
      "text": "On May 26, 2010, the Boeing X-51 Waverider, a scramjet-powered vehicle, completed a successful test flight, reaching speeds of Mach 5 for over 200 seconds.",
      "date": "2010-05-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Longest Scramjet Flight",
      "text": "On May 1, 2013, the X-51A Waverider achieved the longest scramjet-powered flight, flying for over 6 minutes and reaching speeds of Mach 5.1.",
      "date": "2013-05-01"
    }
  ]
}

