      "headline": "John Brown's Raid on Harpers Ferry Begins",
      "text": "On October 16, 1859, abolitionist John Brown led a group of 21 men in a raid on the federal armory at Harpers Ferry, Virginia. The goal was to initiate a slave revolt in the Southern states.",
      "date": "1859-10-16"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Raiders Capture the Armory",
      "text": "By the morning of October 17, 1859, Brown and his men had captured the armory and taken hostages, including Colonel Lewis Washington, a great-grandnephew of George Washington.",
      "date": "1859-10-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Local Militia and U.S. Marines Respond",
      "text": "On October 17, 1859, local militia and U.S. Marines, led by Colonel Robert E. Lee, were dispatched to Harpers Ferry to suppress the raid.",
      "date": "1859-10-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Raid Ends with Brown's Capture",
      "text": "On October 18, 1859, the raid ended when U.S. Marines stormed the armory and captured John Brown. Ten of Brown's men were killed during the raid.",
      "date": "1859-10-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "John Brown's Trial and Execution",
      "text": "John Brown was tried for treason against the Commonwealth of Virginia, murder, and inciting a slave insurrection. He was found guilty and was executed by hanging on December 2, 1859.",
      "date": "1859-12-02"
    }
  ]
}

