      "headline": "Origins of Math Rock",
      "text": "Math rock originated in the late 1980s, drawing influences from progressive rock bands like King Crimson and Rush. The genre is characterized by complex rhythms, odd time signatures, and intricate guitar work.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Influence of King Crimson",
      "text": "King Crimson, a progressive rock band formed in 1968, is often cited as a major influence on the development of math rock due to their use of complex time signatures and intricate musical compositions.",
      "date": "1968"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Influence of Rush",
      "text": "Rush, a Canadian rock band formed in 1968, also influenced math rock with their progressive rock style, characterized by complex compositions and time signatures.",
      "date": "1968"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Math Rock in the 1990s",
      "text": "The 1990s saw the rise of math rock bands such as Slint, Don Caballero, and Shellac, who further defined the genre with their experimental approaches to rhythm and song structure.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Math Rock and Post-Rock",
      "text": "Math rock shares similarities with post-rock, a genre that emerged in the 1990s, but is distinguished by its more 'rocky' approach to songwriting and instrumentation.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Math Rock",
      "text": "In the 2000s and 2010s, math rock continued to evolve with bands like Battles and Tera Melos, who incorporated electronic elements and further pushed the boundaries of the genre.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

