      "headline": "Foundation of Piraeus",
      "text": "Piraeus was founded in the early 5th century BCE as the main port of Athens. Its development was part of Themistocles' plan to make Athens a major naval power.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Construction of the Long Walls",
      "text": "Around 461 BCE, the construction of the Long Walls began, connecting Athens to Piraeus and ensuring safe passage between the city and its port.",
      "date": "-0461"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Salamis",
      "text": "In 480 BCE, the naval Battle of Salamis took place near Piraeus, where the Greek fleet, led by Themistocles, defeated the Persian navy.",
      "date": "-0480"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Destruction by the Spartans",
      "text": "In 404 BCE, after the defeat of Athens in the Peloponnesian War, the Spartans destroyed the Long Walls and the fortifications of Piraeus.",
      "date": "-0404"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reconstruction of Piraeus",
      "text": "In 393 BCE, the Athenian general Conon rebuilt the Long Walls and restored Piraeus as a fortified port.",
      "date": "-0393"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Roman Era",
      "text": "During the Roman period, Piraeus continued to serve as an important port, though its significance declined as the Roman Empire expanded.",
      "date": "-00146"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Byzantine and Ottoman Periods",
      "text": "Throughout the Byzantine and Ottoman periods, Piraeus remained a small fishing village with limited activity.",
      "date": "1453"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Development",
      "text": "In the 19th century, following Greek independence, Piraeus began to grow rapidly as a major port and industrial center.",
      "date": "1834"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Piraeus during World War II",
      "text": "Piraeus was heavily bombed during World War II, causing significant damage to its infrastructure and port facilities.",
      "date": "1941"
