      "text": "In 1961, psychologist Stanley Milgram conducted an experiment at Yale University to study obedience to authority figures, which involved subjects believing they were administering electric shocks to others.",
      "date": "1961"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Stanford Prison Experiment",
      "text": "In 1971, the Stanford Prison Experiment was conducted by Philip Zimbardo to study the psychological effects of perceived power, focusing on the struggle between prisoners and prison officers.",
      "date": "1971"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tuskegee Syphilis Study Ends",
      "text": "The Tuskegee Syphilis Study was officially ended in 1972 after it was exposed by the press, leading to public outrage and significant ethical reforms in research practices.",
      "date": "1972"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Belmont Report Published",
      "text": "In 1979, the Belmont Report was published, outlining ethical principles and guidelines for research involving human subjects, significantly influencing the conduct of future research.",
      "date": "1979"
    }
  ]
}

