      "headline": "Theft of Radioactive Source",
      "text": "On September 13, 1987, a radioactive source was stolen from an abandoned hospital site in Goiânia, Goiás, Brazil. The source was a small capsule containing highly radioactive cesium-137.",
      "date": "1987-09-13"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of Radioactive Material",
      "text": "On September 18, 1987, the radioactive material was discovered by a junkyard owner who noticed a glowing blue light coming from the capsule.",
      "date": "1987-09-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Death",
      "text": "On October 23, 1987, the first death occurred as a result of the radioactive contamination. A 6-year-old girl who had been exposed to the cesium-137 succumbed to radiation sickness.",
      "date": "1987-10-23"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Second Death",
      "text": "On October 28, 1987, another victim died due to radiation exposure. This was the second of four deaths directly attributed to the Goiânia accident.",
      "date": "1987-10-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Third Death",
      "text": "On October 29, 1987, a third person died from radiation exposure related to the Goiânia accident.",
      "date": "1987-10-29"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fourth Death",
      "text": "On November 6, 1987, the fourth and final death occurred as a result of the Goiânia accident.",
      "date": "1987-11-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mass Examination for Contamination",
      "text": "Following the discovery of the radioactive contamination, approximately 112,000 people were examined for radioactive contamination, and 249 were found to have been contaminated.",
      "date": "1987"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decontamination Efforts",
      "text": "Extensive decontamination efforts were undertaken in Goiânia to clean up the radioactive material and prevent further exposure.",
      "date": "1987"
    }
  ]
}

