      "headline": "Agony in the Garden",
      "text": "According to the New Testament, Jesus Christ underwent the Agony in the Garden of Gethsemane, where he prayed and expressed his anguish before his arrest. This event is traditionally dated to the night before his crucifixion, around 30-33 AD.",
      "date": "0030"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arrest of Jesus",
      "text": "In the Garden of Gethsemane, Jesus was arrested by the temple guards after being betrayed by Judas Iscariot. This event is a significant moment in the Passion of Christ, traditionally dated to the night before his crucifixion, around 30-33 AD.",
      "date": "0030"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Construction of Churches",
      "text": "Throughout history, several churches have been constructed at or near the site of Gethsemane to commemorate its significance in Christianity. The earliest known church was built in the 4th century AD by the Byzantine Empire.",
      "date": "0400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Destruction and Reconstruction",
      "text": "The original Byzantine church was destroyed in 614 AD during the Persian invasion. It was later rebuilt and has undergone several reconstructions over the centuries.",
      "date": "0614"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Current Church of All Nations",
      "text": "The current Church of All Nations, also known as the Basilica of the Agony, was completed in 1924. It stands on the site of the earlier Byzantine and Crusader churches and is a major pilgrimage site.",
      "date": "1924"
    }
  ]
}

