{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "First Patent for a Typing Machine",
      "text": "In 1714, the first patent for a typing machine was issued to Henry Mill, an English engineer. The patent described a machine that could produce letters on paper, but it is unclear if a working model was ever built.",
      "date": "1714"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Invention of the Typographer",
      "text": "In 1829, William Austin Burt patented the 'Typographer', which is considered the first documented typewriter in the United States. However, it was cumbersome and not commercially successful.",
      "date": "1829"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Hansen Writing Ball",
      "text": "In 1865, Rasmus Malling-Hansen of Denmark invented the Hansen Writing Ball, which became the first commercially produced typewriter. It was used in offices across Europe.",
      "date": "1865"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sholes and Glidden Typewriter",
      "text": "In 1868, Christopher Latham Sholes, along with Samuel Soule and Carlos Glidden, patented the Sholes and Glidden typewriter. It was the first device to be called a typewriter and the first to be commercially successful.",
      "date": "1868"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the QWERTY Keyboard",
      "text": "In 1873, the Sholes and Glidden typewriter, also known as the Remington No. 1, was introduced with the QWERTY keyboard layout, which is still in use today.",
      "date": "1873"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Electric Typewriter",
      "text": "In 1902, the first electric typewriter was patented by Thomas Edison, although it was not commercially viable at the time.",
      "date": "1902"
    },
    {
      "headline": "IBM Selectric Typewriter",
      "text": "In 1961, IBM introduced the Selectric typewriter, which replaced the traditional typebars with a spherical type element, or 'typeball', that could be easily changed to alter fonts.",
