      "headline": "Beginning of the Crisis in Thailand",
      "text": "The Asian financial crisis began in Thailand with the financial collapse of the Thai baht after the Thai government was forced to float the baht on July 2, 1997, due to lack of foreign currency to support its fixed exchange rate.",
      "date": "1997-07-02"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spread of the Crisis",
      "text": "Following the collapse of the Thai baht, the financial crisis spread to other Asian countries, including Indonesia, South Korea, and Malaysia, causing severe economic disruptions.",
      "date": "1997-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "IMF Intervention",
      "text": "In response to the crisis, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) intervened with financial assistance packages for affected countries, including a $40 billion program for Indonesia in November 1997.",
      "date": "1997-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "South Korea's Economic Turmoil",
      "text": "South Korea was hit hard by the crisis, leading to a $58 billion bailout package from the IMF in December 1997, the largest in IMF history at the time.",
      "date": "1997-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Economic Recovery Begins",
      "text": "By 1998, affected countries began to show signs of recovery, aided by IMF programs and structural reforms, with economic growth resuming in many regions by 1999.",
      "date": "1998"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Crisis",
      "text": "The rapid recovery of the affected economies by 1999 marked the end of the Asian financial crisis, with most countries experiencing economic growth and stabilization.",
      "date": "1999"
    }
  ]
}

