      "text": "The Kingdom of Dahomey was founded around 1600 on the Abomey Plateau by the Fon people.",
      "date": "1600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion under King Houegbadja",
      "text": "King Houegbadja, who ruled from 1645 to 1685, expanded the kingdom and established the capital at Abomey.",
      "date": "1645"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of King Agaja",
      "text": "King Agaja ruled from 1708 to 1740 and significantly expanded Dahomey by conquering the coastal Kingdom of Whydah in 1727, gaining access to the Atlantic Slave Trade.",
      "date": "1708"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Conquest of Whydah",
      "text": "In 1727, King Agaja conquered the Kingdom of Whydah, which was a major turning point for Dahomey as it provided access to the Atlantic coast and the slave trade.",
      "date": "1727"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of King Ghezo",
      "text": "King Ghezo ruled from 1818 to 1858 and is known for his military reforms and efforts to end Dahomey's dependence on the slave trade.",
      "date": "1818"
    },
    {
      "headline": "French-Dahomean Wars",
      "text": "The French-Dahomean Wars occurred between 1890 and 1894, leading to the eventual colonization of Dahomey by France.",
      "date": "1890"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Colonization by France",
      "text": "In 1894, after the French-Dahomean Wars, Dahomey became a French protectorate, marking the end of the kingdom's independence.",
      "date": "1894"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formal Annexation by France",
      "text": "In 1904, the Kingdom of Dahomey was formally annexed into the French colonial empire as part of French West Africa.",
      "date": "1904"
    }
  ]
}

