      "text": "Carson City was founded in 1858 as a stopover for California-bound immigrants. It was named after the mountain man Kit Carson.",
      "date": "1858"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Comstock Lode Discovery",
      "text": "The discovery of the Comstock Lode in 1859, a major silver strike in the mountains to the northeast, contributed to the development of Carson City.",
      "date": "1859"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Carson City Becomes Nevada's Capital",
      "text": "Carson City was designated as the capital of the Nevada Territory in 1861 and continued as the state capital when Nevada was admitted to the Union in 1864.",
      "date": "1864"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Virginia and Truckee Railroad",
      "text": "Carson City became a hub for the Virginia and Truckee Railroad, which was completed in 1870 and played a significant role in the city's economy.",
      "date": "1870"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Removal of Railroad Tracks",
      "text": "The tracks of the Virginia and Truckee Railroad were removed in 1950, marking the end of an era for Carson City as a railroad hub.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Consolidation as an Independent City",
      "text": "Carson City was consolidated as an independent city in 1969, merging with Ormsby County to form a single municipal government.",
      "date": "1969"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Population in 2020 Census",
      "text": "As of the 2020 census, Carson City had a population of 58,639, making it the 6th most populous city in Nevada.",
      "date": "2020"
    }
  ]
}

