      "headline": "Outbreak of King Philip's War",
      "text": "The war began in June 1675 when tensions between the Wampanoag tribe, led by Metacom (King Philip), and the English settlers erupted into violence.",
      "date": "1675-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Attack on Swansea",
      "text": "On June 20, 1675, the first attack of King Philip's War occurred when Wampanoag warriors attacked the town of Swansea in Plymouth Colony.",
      "date": "1675-06-20"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Bloody Brook",
      "text": "On September 18, 1675, a group of colonial militia was ambushed and defeated by Native American forces at Bloody Brook, near present-day Deerfield, Massachusetts.",
      "date": "1675-09-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Great Swamp Fight",
      "text": "On December 19, 1675, colonial forces attacked a fortified Narragansett village in what is now South Kingstown, Rhode Island, resulting in heavy casualties for the Narragansett.",
      "date": "1675-12-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Attack on Lancaster",
      "text": "On February 10, 1676, Native American forces attacked the town of Lancaster, Massachusetts, capturing or killing many settlers, including Mary Rowlandson, who later wrote a famous captivity narrative.",
      "date": "1676-02-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of King Philip",
      "text": "On August 12, 1676, Metacom (King Philip) was killed by colonial forces in present-day Bristol, Rhode Island, effectively ending major hostilities in southern New England.",
      "date": "1676-08-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Hostilities in Northern New England",
      "text": "The war continued in northern New England until the signing of the Treaty of Casco Bay on April 12, 1678, which marked the official end of King Philip's War.",
      "date": "1678-04-12"
    }
  ]
}

