      "headline": "Screenplay for 'Thirty Seconds Over Tokyo'",
      "text": "In 1944, Trumbo wrote the screenplay for 'Thirty Seconds Over Tokyo', which became a major success.",
      "date": "1944"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Refusal to Testify Before HUAC",
      "text": "On October 28, 1947, Dalton Trumbo refused to testify before the House Un-American Activities Committee (HUAC) during its investigation of Communist influences in Hollywood.",
      "date": "1947-10-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Imprisonment for Contempt of Congress",
      "text": "In 1950, Trumbo was sentenced to 11 months in prison for contempt of Congress after refusing to testify before HUAC.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Blacklisted in Hollywood",
      "text": "Following his imprisonment, Trumbo was blacklisted in Hollywood, forcing him to write under pseudonyms.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Screenplay for 'Roman Holiday'",
      "text": "Trumbo wrote the screenplay for 'Roman Holiday' (1953) under the pseudonym Ian McLellan Hunter, which won an Academy Award.",
      "date": "1953"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Screenplay for 'The Brave One'",
      "text": "In 1956, Trumbo wrote the screenplay for 'The Brave One' under the pseudonym Robert Rich, which won an Academy Award.",
      "date": "1956"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Screenplay for 'Spartacus'",
      "text": "Dalton Trumbo wrote the screenplay for 'Spartacus' (1960), marking the end of the Hollywood blacklist.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Screenplay for 'Exodus'",
      "text": "Trumbo wrote the screenplay for 'Exodus' (1960), further solidifying his return to Hollywood.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Public Acknowledgment of Work",
      "text": "In 1960, Trumbo was publicly acknowledged for his work on 'Spartacus' and 'Exodus', effectively ending the blacklist.",
      "date": "1960"
