      "headline": "Foundation of the First Bulgarian Empire",
      "text": "The First Bulgarian Empire was founded in 680–681 after the Bulgars, led by Asparuh, moved south to the northeastern Balkans. They secured Byzantine recognition of their right to settle south of the Danube by defeating the Byzantine army led by Constantine IV.",
      "date": "0681"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of Khan Krum",
      "text": "Khan Krum ruled from 803 to 814 and expanded the empire significantly. He is known for his military successes against the Byzantine Empire, including the capture of the Byzantine capital, Pliska, in 811.",
      "date": "0803"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Pliska",
      "text": "In 811, Khan Krum defeated the Byzantine Emperor Nicephorus I at the Battle of Pliska, a significant victory that solidified Bulgarian power in the region.",
      "date": "0811"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Christianization of Bulgaria",
      "text": "Under the reign of Boris I, Bulgaria officially adopted Christianity in 864, which helped to integrate the diverse population and establish stronger ties with the Byzantine Empire.",
      "date": "0864"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of Simeon I",
      "text": "Simeon I ruled from 893 to 927 and presided over the First Bulgarian Empire's Golden Age. During his reign, the empire reached its greatest territorial extent and cultural development.",
      "date": "0893"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Achelous",
      "text": "In 917, the Bulgarians under Simeon I defeated the Byzantine forces at the Battle of Achelous, further asserting their dominance in the Balkans.",
      "date": "0917"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline and Fall of the First Bulgarian Empire",
      "text": "The First Bulgarian Empire gradually declined due to internal strife and external pressures, culminating in its conquest by the Byzantine Empire in 1018.",
      "date": "1018"
    }
  ]
}

