      "headline": "Origin of Shogi",
      "text": "Shogi is believed to have originated from chaturanga, an ancient Indian game, which spread to China and then to Japan. The exact date is uncertain, but it is thought to have been introduced to Japan around the 8th century.",
      "date": "-0700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Recorded Mention",
      "text": "The first recorded mention of shogi in Japan is found in the historical text 'Nihon Shoki', which was completed in 720. This suggests that the game was known in Japan by the early 8th century.",
      "date": "0720"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Modern Shogi",
      "text": "The modern form of shogi began to take shape during the Heian period (794-1185), with the introduction of the drop rule, which allows captured pieces to be returned to the board as one's own.",
      "date": "0794"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of Shogi Variants",
      "text": "During the 16th century, various forms of shogi were played, including large board variants. The standard 9x9 board became the most popular.",
      "date": "1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tokugawa Shogunate and Shogi",
      "text": "During the Tokugawa shogunate (1603-1868), shogi became a popular pastime among the samurai class. The shogunate also established the first official shogi schools.",
      "date": "1603"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Professional Shogi Players",
      "text": "In the Edo period, the first professional shogi players emerged, and the title of Meijin (Master) was established.",
      "date": "1612"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Shogi Tournaments",
      "text": "The first official shogi tournaments were held in the late 19th century, marking the beginning of organized competitive play.",
      "date": "1890"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of Japan Shogi Association",
      "text": "The Japan Shogi Association was founded in 1924 to promote and organize shogi activities, including professional tournaments.",
      "date": "1924"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Women's Professional Shogi",
      "text": "In 1974, the first women's professional shogi league was established, providing a platform for female players to compete professionally.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Computer Shogi Development",
      "text": "The development of computer shogi programs began in the 1970s, with significant advancements in artificial intelligence leading to competitive matches against human players by the 21st century.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Historic Computer Victory",
      "text": "In 2013, a computer shogi program defeated a professional shogi player for the first time in a public match, marking a significant milestone in AI development.",
      "date": "2013"
    }
  ]
}

