      "text": "The Stone of Scone was first used in the coronation of Scottish monarchs. Its use dates back to ancient times, but it became a symbol of Scottish kingship by the 9th century.",
      "date": "-0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Stone Moved to England",
      "text": "In 1296, during the reign of King Edward I of England, the Stone of Scone was taken from Scotland and moved to Westminster Abbey in London. It was placed in the Coronation Chair, where it remained for centuries.",
      "date": "1296"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Coronation of English and British Monarchs",
      "text": "After being moved to Westminster Abbey, the Stone of Scone was used in the coronation of English and later British monarchs, symbolizing the union of the Scottish and English crowns.",
      "date": "1296"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Stone Returned to Scotland",
      "text": "On November 30, 1996, the Stone of Scone was returned to Scotland. It was placed in Edinburgh Castle, where it remains on display alongside the Scottish Crown Jewels.",
