      "headline": "Founding of the French Communist Party",
      "text": "The French Communist Party (PCF) was founded on December 29, 1920, during the Tours Congress of the French Section of the Workers' International (SFIO). The majority of the SFIO decided to join the Communist International, leading to the creation of the PCF.",
      "date": "1920-12-29"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Participation in the Popular Front",
      "text": "In 1936, the French Communist Party became part of the Popular Front, a coalition of left-wing parties that won the French general elections. This led to the formation of a government under Léon Blum.",
      "date": "1936"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War II and the Resistance",
      "text": "During World War II, after initially being banned following the Nazi-Soviet Pact, the PCF became active in the French Resistance against the German occupation after the German invasion of the Soviet Union in 1941.",
      "date": "1941"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-War Influence",
      "text": "After World War II, the French Communist Party became one of the largest political parties in France, participating in the government from 1944 to 1947.",
      "date": "1944"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline in Influence",
      "text": "The influence of the French Communist Party began to decline in the late 1970s and 1980s, as it lost ground to the Socialist Party and other political movements.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Joining the Party of the European Left",
      "text": "In 2004, the French Communist Party became a founding member of the Party of the European Left, a political party at the European level.",
      "date": "2004"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Participation in the European Parliament",
      "text": "The French Communist Party's Members of the European Parliament (MEPs) sit with The Left in the European Parliament – GUE/NGL group.",
      "date": "2004"
    }
  ]
}

