      "headline": "IBM System/360 Announcement",
      "text": "On April 7, 1964, IBM announced the System/360, a family of mainframe computer systems that revolutionized the computer industry by allowing customers to upgrade to more powerful models without having to rewrite their applications.",
      "date": "1964-04-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Virtual Memory",
      "text": "In the 1970s, mainframe computers began to incorporate virtual memory, which allowed them to handle larger and more complex applications by simulating additional memory.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the IBM System/370",
      "text": "In 1970, IBM introduced the System/370, which included improvements such as semiconductor memory and support for virtual memory, enhancing the capabilities of mainframe computers.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Parallel Sysplex",
      "text": "In 1994, IBM introduced Parallel Sysplex, a technology that allowed multiple mainframe systems to work together as a single system, improving scalability and reliability.",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mainframe Computers in the 21st Century",
      "text": "In the 2000s and beyond, mainframe computers continued to evolve with advancements in processing power, storage capacity, and connectivity, maintaining their role in critical enterprise applications.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

