      "headline": "Birth of Nur Jahan",
      "text": "Nur Jahan, originally named Mehr-un-Nissa, was born in 1577 in Kandahar, which is in present-day Afghanistan.",
      "date": "1577"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Marriage to Sher Afghan",
      "text": "In 1594, Mehr-un-Nissa married Sher Afghan, a Persian nobleman serving under the Mughal Empire.",
      "date": "1594"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widowhood and Entry into Jahangir's Court",
      "text": "After the death of her husband Sher Afghan in 1607, Mehr-un-Nissa entered the Mughal court and became a lady-in-waiting to Empress Ruqaiya Sultan Begum.",
      "date": "1607"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Marriage to Emperor Jahangir",
      "text": "Mehr-un-Nissa married Emperor Jahangir on May 25, 1611, and was given the title 'Nur Jahan', meaning 'Light of the World'.",
      "date": "1611-05-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Influence in the Mughal Court",
      "text": "From 1611 onwards, Nur Jahan became highly influential in the Mughal court, effectively ruling the empire alongside Jahangir.",
      "date": "1611"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Political and Cultural Contributions",
      "text": "During her time as Empress, Nur Jahan was known for her political acumen, patronage of the arts, and architectural contributions, including the construction of several gardens and buildings.",
      "date": "1611"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Jahangir",
      "text": "Emperor Jahangir died in 1627, which led to a power struggle in the Mughal court.",
      "date": "1627"
