      "headline": "Holocene Extinction",
      "text": "The current Holocene extinction, also known as the sixth mass extinction, is being caused by human technology and activities. The effects of this extinction event may last for up to five million years.",
      "date": "0000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Increase in Sun's Luminosity",
      "text": "Over the next billion years, the Sun's luminosity is expected to increase by about 10%. This increase will result in a hotter climate on Earth, potentially causing the oceans to evaporate and making the planet uninhabitable for life as we know it.",
      "date": "1000000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cooling of Earth's Interior",
      "text": "Over geological timescales, Earth's interior will continue to cool. This cooling process will eventually lead to the solidification of the outer core, which could stop the geodynamo and result in the loss of Earth's magnetic field.",
      "date": "2000000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gravitational Interactions",
      "text": "Gravitational interactions with other objects in the Solar System, such as the Moon, will continue to affect Earth's rotation and orbit. Over billions of years, these interactions could lead to significant changes in Earth's axial tilt and orbital eccentricity.",
      "date": "3000000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sun's Red Giant Phase",
      "text": "In approximately 5 billion years, the Sun will enter its red giant phase. During this phase, the Sun will expand significantly, potentially engulfing the inner planets, including Earth, and marking the end of the planet as we know it.",
      "date": "5000000000"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
