      "headline": "Introduction of Combined Statistical Areas",
      "text": "Combined Statistical Areas (CSAs) were first designated by the United States Office of Management and Budget (OMB) in 2003. They are defined as a combination of adjacent metropolitan and micropolitan statistical areas that demonstrate economic or social linkage.",
      "date": "2003"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Criteria for CSA Designation",
      "text": "The OMB defines a CSA by various combinations of adjacent metropolitan and micropolitan areas with economic ties measured by commuting patterns. This allows for a more comprehensive understanding of regional economies and social linkages.",
      "date": "2003"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Retention of Individual Designations",
      "text": "Despite being part of a larger Combined Statistical Area, each metropolitan or micropolitan statistical area retains its own designation within the CSA. This ensures that the unique characteristics and data of each area are preserved.",
      "date": "2003"
    }
  ]
}

