      "text": "The first known anti-miscegenation law in what would become the United States was enacted in the colony of Virginia in 1691. It prohibited marriage between whites and non-whites.",
      "date": "1691"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Maryland Enacts Anti-Miscegenation Law",
      "text": "In 1692, Maryland passed a law prohibiting marriage between white women and black men, reflecting the racial attitudes of the time.",
      "date": "1692"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Pennsylvania Repeals Anti-Miscegenation Law",
      "text": "In 1780, Pennsylvania became one of the first states to repeal its anti-miscegenation law, as part of a broader movement towards abolitionism.",
      "date": "1780"
    },
    {
      "headline": "California Enacts Anti-Miscegenation Law",
      "text": "California enacted its anti-miscegenation law in 1850, prohibiting marriages between whites and blacks, mulattoes, and Native Americans.",
      "date": "1850"
    },
    {
      "headline": "California Repeals Anti-Miscegenation Law",
      "text": "In 1948, California's Supreme Court ruled in Perez v. Sharp that the state's anti-miscegenation law was unconstitutional, making California the first state to do so.",
      "date": "1948"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Loving v. Virginia Decision",
      "text": "On June 12, 1967, the U.S. Supreme Court ruled in Loving v. Virginia that all state laws prohibiting interracial marriage were unconstitutional, effectively ending all race-based legal restrictions on marriage in the United States.",
      "date": "1967-06-12"
    }
  ]
}

