      "headline": "Start of Demolition Activities",
      "text": "The demolition of early Islamic heritage sites in Saudi Arabia began as part of the expansion projects for the Masjid al-Haram in Mecca and the Prophet's Mosque in Medina. This process has been ongoing for several decades, with significant activities noted from the late 20th century onwards.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Demolition of the Birthplace of Muhammad",
      "text": "In the 1980s, the site believed to be the birthplace of the Islamic prophet Muhammad was demolished to make way for a library, as part of the urban development projects in Mecca.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion of the Masjid al-Haram",
      "text": "Throughout the 1990s and 2000s, the Masjid al-Haram in Mecca underwent significant expansion, leading to the demolition of several historical sites associated with early Islam.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Destruction of the Ottoman-Era Ajyad Fortress",
      "text": "In 2002, the Ajyad Fortress, an Ottoman-era structure, was demolished to make way for the Abraj Al Bait Towers, a large hotel complex near the Masjid al-Haram.",
      "date": "2002"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Continued Expansion and Demolition",
      "text": "The 2010s saw continued expansion of the holy sites in Mecca and Medina, with further demolitions of historical sites to accommodate the growing number of pilgrims.",
      "date": "2010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Demolition of the House of Khadijah",
      "text": "In 2012, the house believed to have belonged to Khadijah, the first wife of Muhammad, was demolished during the expansion of the Masjid al-Haram.",
      "date": "2012"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ongoing Demolition Activities",
      "text": "As of the 2020s, demolition activities continue as part of the Saudi government's efforts to expand and modernize the infrastructure surrounding the holy sites in Mecca and Medina.",
      "date": "2020"
    }
  ]
}

