{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Historical Legal Acceptance",
      "text": "Historically, marital rape was not recognized as a crime in many societies. The concept of a husband's right to sexual access to his wife was legally accepted in many cultures for centuries.",
      "date": "-0400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Legal Challenge",
      "text": "In 1736, Sir Matthew Hale, an English jurist, declared that a husband cannot be guilty of raping his wife, a legal precedent that influenced many legal systems.",
      "date": "1736"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Legal Reforms",
      "text": "In the mid-20th century, feminist movements began to challenge the legal acceptance of marital rape, advocating for the recognition of women's rights within marriage.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Country to Criminalize Marital Rape",
      "text": "In 1976, Nebraska became the first U.S. state to remove the marital rape exemption, making it illegal for a husband to rape his wife.",
      "date": "1976"
    },
    {
      "headline": "International Recognition",
      "text": "In 1993, the United Nations Declaration on the Elimination of Violence Against Women recognized marital rape as a human rights violation.",
      "date": "1993"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Global Legal Reforms",
      "text": "By the early 21st century, many countries around the world had enacted laws criminalizing marital rape, although enforcement and recognition vary widely.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ongoing Legal and Cultural Challenges",
      "text": "Despite legal reforms, marital rape remains a contentious issue in many cultures, with ongoing debates about consent, marital rights, and enforcement of laws.",
      "date": "2023"
    }
  ]
}

